<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Igor Stravinsky</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1882">1882</a>–<a href="/years/1971">1971</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Igor Fyodorovich Stravinsky</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Igor Fyodorovich Stravinsky was a Russian composer and conductor, a French citizen from 1934 and an American one from 1945, and a central figure in modernist music. Born in Oranienbaum near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, he studied under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolai-rimsky-korsakov">Nikolai Rimsky-Korsakov</a> until 1908. For Sergei Diaghilev's Ballets Russes he wrote The Firebird (1910), Petrushka (1911) and The Rite of Spring (1913), whose premiere caused a near-riot. His career is divided into a Russian period, a neoclassical period (1920–1951) and a serial period (1954–1968), in which he took up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnold-schoenberg">Arnold Schoenberg</a>'s twelve-tone technique. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City in 1971.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/igor-stravinsky.jpg" alt="Igor Stravinsky" width="330" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Grantham Bain Collection · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Igor_Stravinsky_LOC_32392u.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In music Arnold Schoenberg devised a way of organising sound by twelve-note rows, <b>Igor Stravinsky</b> shocked Paris with The Rite of Spring before turning to a neoclassical style, and Alban Berg and Béla Bartók followed their own paths.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · People</a></li><li><blockquote>In April 1917, after Tsar Nicholas II had abdicated, Sergei Diaghilev asked <b>Igor Stravinsky</b> to orchestrate it for wind instruments to open a concert that would otherwise have begun with the anthem &quot;God Save the Tsar&quot;; Stravinsky worked through the night, helped by Lord Berners and Ernest Ansermet.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-song-of-the-volga-boatmen">The Song of the Volga Boatmen</a></li></ul></section>
